\FloatBarrier
\subsection{AI4AI: Automated Modeling for Scientific Discovery}

\textit{[Validated Prototype]} AI-for-AI (AI4AI) refers to a design paradigm in which agents read, distill, and recombine algorithmic building blocks from existing codebases into new machine learning models. Rather than treating model design as a one-shot prompt, AI4AI treats it as an iterative design loop: an agent reads external repositories, extracts reusable algorithm atoms (e.g., attention mechanisms, distribution losses, latent regularizers), maps them to the target project's training interface, composes a new model, and submits training, inference, and evaluation jobs through a computing-platform job-submission system. Evaluation metrics then feed back into the next design cycle.

We exercised this paradigm on cell perturbation prediction---the task of predicting how a cell's transcriptome changes in response to a genetic or chemical perturbation---using a NeMo/BioNeMo training stack supporting PBMC, Replogle, and Tahoe benchmark datasets. The agent distilled algorithm atoms from the \texttt{stack} single-cell foundation model repository, producing structured design cards covering Cell-by-Gene Tabular Attention, Rectangular Gene Masking, Distribution Heads, Sliced Wasserstein Regularization, and In-Context Generation. These atoms were mapped to the project's NeMo model interface (MInterface, DInterface) and composed into new perturbation-prediction architectures under a frozen RJOB submission contract that forbids ad-hoc code changes during job submission.

Delta PCC---the improvement in Pearson correlation over a baseline JIT model---was tracked across design iterations. In the recorded pilot, Delta PCC improved from 0.30 (Iteration~1) to 0.48, 0.58, and finally 0.64 (Iteration~4), demonstrating that iterative atom-level design recombination can yield measurable performance gains (Fig.~\ref{fig:ai4ai-delta-pcc}). The complete agent logs, design cards, model code, configurations, and evaluation records are archived at \url{https://github.com/BruthYU/CELL_AI4AI}.

\textit{Limitations:} The Delta PCC curve represents a single pilot trajectory with four design iterations; statistical significance, cross-seed stability, and generalization to new perturbation tasks have not been established. The algorithm-atom distillation quality depends on the agent's reading fidelity and the target repository's code organization. The current pilot uses post-hoc metric reporting without pre-registered stopping criteria or blinded evaluation. Formal validation would require multi-seed replication, pre-registered success thresholds, and independent re-execution on held-out perturbation types.

\begin{figure}[ht!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{figures/ai4ai-delta-pcc.png}
\caption{Delta PCC improvement across AI4AI design iterations for cell perturbation prediction. The curve tracks iterative algorithm-atom recombination on PBMC, Replogle, and Tahoe datasets under a NeMo/BioNeMo training stack.}
\label{fig:ai4ai-delta-pcc}
\end{figure}

